OpenH264 Video Codec provided by Cisco Systems, Inc.

Suno's software H.264 encoder and decoder (GStreamer's openh264enc and
openh264dec, and FFmpeg's libopenh264) use Cisco's OpenH264 binary. Suno does
not include, build or redistribute that binary: on each device it is
downloaded directly from Cisco (ciscobinary.openh264.org), checked against the
SHA-256 recorded in /usr/share/Suno-openh264/cisco-openh264.conf, and stored in
/var/lib/openh264. Flatpak applications get the same file through the
org.projectSuno.Platform.openh264 extension, whose download also comes from
Cisco. Cisco's license for the binary, including the conditions that apply to
software using it, is at https://www.openh264.org/BINARY_LICENSE.txt.

You control whether the binary is used:

  Suno-openh264 status     show what is downloaded and whether it is used
  Suno-openh264 disable    remove it from this computer and its Flatpak runtime
  Suno-openh264 enable     download it from Cisco again and use it

When it is disabled or not yet downloaded, H.264 encoding falls back to the
hardware encoders (VA-API) or to other codecs, and openh264enc reports that it
cannot start. Applications that were already running use a newly downloaded
binary after they restart.
